Well, the current version is 3.5, and it's just D&D (no A).
If you go to a store and buy new books they're probably
going to be 3.5.

They use a rules system which is called "the d20 system", a lot
of which you can view online at:
http://www.opengamingfoundation.org/
(follow the D20 3.5 SRD link).

The Player's Handbook (PHB) is the most important of all the
books by at least a factor of 100. Everyone who wants
to play should buy one including the DM. Make some
characters, absorb the combat rules, and you're ready to
play. You don't *really* need any other books.

However if your DM is not a prodigy of industrious
creativity there are other books to buy. The Monster
Manual is important, and some sample adventures to
run the party through and use as a template for your
own creations. In my experience with 3E, making
the monsters was a big pain in the ass since they are
as fully statted out as PCs.

I used to like Dungeon magazine for ideas, but I'm
not sure if it's still good or even published at all.

This used to be an excellent site for all things D20:
http://www.enworld.org/
It might still be. The bulletin boards there might
be a good place to go for help -- better than
r.g.r.n. at least :)

There are plenty of other games besides D&D 3E, and
the D20 system has lots of annoyances. Still, it's probably
your best bet for starting off in heroic fantasy gaming.

